TAR(1)                        User Commands

NAME
    tar - create, list and extract ustar archives

SYNOPSIS
    tar [-C dir] [-z] -c [-v] [-f file] path...
    tar [-C dir] [-z] -t [-v] [-f file]
    tar [-C dir] [-z] -x [-m] [-p] [-v] [-f file] [member...]

DESCRIPTION
    Writes the named files and directories, recursively, into an archive
    in the POSIX ustar format (-c), lists the members of an archive (-t)
    or extracts them (-x), all of them or the named ones. This is the tar
    of sbase, the suckless base utilities. Without -f the archive is
    standard output or standard input, so tar -c path | tar -t works.
    -C changes to dir before reading or writing files. -z compresses and
    decompresses through gzip in a child that tar does not wait for, so
    an archive written with -z may still be in progress when tar exits; -j, -J and -Z name bzip2, xz and compress,
    which minios does not have. -v prints each member name. -m sets the
    time of extracted files to the current time instead of the recorded
    one; -p keeps the recorded permissions (they are stored but not
    enforced). -h follows symbolic links when archiving; there are none.

    Names longer than 100 characters use the ustar prefix field, up to 255
    characters. Archives written by the GNU and BSD tar programs in the
    ustar format are read; their extension headers are not.

SEE ALSO
    ar(1), gzip(1), index(7)
